MEMORANDUM
TO:
Governing Body, City of Cheyenne
FROM:
Renee Smith, Economic Resource Administrator
DATE:
AUGUST7,2024
SUBJECT: GRANT AGREEMENT BETWEEN THE US ENDOWMENT FOR FORESTRY
AND COMMUNITIES INNOVATIVE FINA NANCE FOR NATIONAL FORESTS
GRANT AND THE CITY OF CHEYENNE
The City of Cheyenne in partnership with the Cheyenne Board of Public Utilities has been working
collaborative ly with various public andprivate agencies to protect our source water andreduce wildfire risk.
Partners include theUS Forest Service, National Forest Foundation, Wyoming Association of Rural Water
Systems, World Resources Institute, and thetown of Encampment.
communities, including the cities of Cheyenne and Encampment.Funding and capacity constraints at theUSFS
limit its ability to rapidly address this risk.The partners are exploring the first-of-its-kind use of Wyoming
State Revolving Fund Loans to finance large-scaleforest restoration.To evaluate the feasibility of the model,
this grant will provide funds to support collaboration, community engagement, and legal services to determine
the feasibility of using the State'sR LF to finance forest restoration in the Medicine Bow-Rout t National Forests
&Thunder Basin National Grassland(MBRTB).
This innovative pilot could revolutionize national forest management funding strategies by building on the
2010 initiative by Arizona's Salt River Project, which similarly used these loans for watershed and forest
conservation to protect against wildfire risks.Given the age of this precedent, a renewed proof of concept is
crucialto demonstrate the current applicability and benefits of such funding strategies, potentially encouraging
their broader adoption across the U.S.
management to mitigate wildfire risks, and (2) the limited financial capabilities of nearby communities to
match USFSfunding.Despite the allocation of nearly$500millionfrom the Wildfire Crisis Strategy tovarious
western US forests for 2024 (USDA), Wyoming's forests were not identified as priority areas.This omission
limits USFS's ability to mitigate wildfire threats and protect essential infrastructure used by municipalities,
water utilities, and communities.Additionally, adjacent communities lack the resources or personnel needed
to supplement federal funds or underpin large-scale loans. By accessing WSRFs for forest restoration and
designing appropriate cost-share agreements with the USFS, we aim to overcome these financial hurdles by
smaller communities.
The MBRTB spans 2.2 million acres, with 40% classified as high fire hazard. The Landscape Vegetation
Analysis(LaVA)project aims toimplement 288,000acres of vegetation management across theMBRTB to
enhance ecosystem resilience, safeguard critical drinking water sources, and protect communities.Capacity
and funding have limited theUSFSto about 4,000 acres per year.At thisrate,80%of the approved treatments
